{
  "stages": {
    "prototype": {
      "level": 4,
      "rationale": "Throwaway code has no maintenance cost, so judging it only by whether it runs is the right trade: the point is to learn something fast and then delete it. Keep real credentials, customer data, and shared infrastructure out of the loop so a bad guess can't escape the sandbox."
    },
    "proof-of-concept": {
      "level": 3,
      "rationale": "A demo has to work on demand in front of stakeholders, so selective review plus behavior tests is worth the modest cost. Full vibe coding here is risky because hardcoded values and fake data can make an idea look more real than it is, and that misleads the decision the demo exists to support."
    },
    "mvp": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "MVP code usually becomes the foundation of the real product, and mistakes in auth, data handling, and schema are far cheaper to catch now than after users depend on them. At this volume line-by-line review is still affordable; reserve level 3 for genuinely disposable slices with no data or security surface."
    },
    "production": {
      "level": 2,
      "rationale": "Paying customers and a maintaining team make every defect expensive, so each AI-written change needs a named human who has read it and can defend it. Level 3 is acceptable only for isolated, low-blast-radius changes behind strong CI, monitoring, and rollback."
    },
    "critical": {
      "level": 1,
      "rationale": "In regulated or safety-critical systems, accountability and traceability cannot be delegated to a model, so AI can suggest approaches and draft tests but a person must write and own every line. Drop to 0 for the highest-risk paths such as control logic, security boundaries, and anything with a formal audit obligation."
    }
  },
  "summary": "Set the level from two questions: how long will this code live, and what does a silent defect cost? Vibe coding (4) is a learning tool, not a shipping tool: it is appropriate only when the code is disposable and failure is cheap, and output from it should never be promoted into a later stage. As you move from prototype to critical, the review burden shifts from \"does it work?\" to \"who is accountable when it doesn't?\", which is why the recommended level falls steadily from 4 to 1."
}

